window.PH_BOOK_LOAD({"id":"10-03","translated":true,"translator":"AI (Claude)","sections":[{"slug":"10-03-01","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-03-01/","title_he":"א – הברכה על הלחם","title_en":"The Blessing over Bread","body_en":"<p>Bread is made from the five species of grain, and therefore seemingly one should have recited over it \"who creates the fruit of the ground\" (Borei Pri Ha-adama), as one recites over all the species that grow from the ground. However, because of its great importance, being the main food of a person, the Sages instituted a special blessing for it: \"Blessed are You, Lord our God, King of the universe, who brings forth bread from the earth.\"</p><p>Bread is a special food, for all the animals in the world eat natural foods such as grasses, leaves, seeds, grains, vegetables, fruits and even meat, whereas man eats bread, which is a food produced through a lengthy process. Many perceive bread as a natural food and do not notice that it is a food whose preparation requires very many labors: plowing the ground, sowing the wheat, reaping the stalks, separating the kernels of grain from the stalks and the chaff by threshing, winnowing and sorting, grinding the kernels into flour, kneading it with water, and baking the dough into bread. One should further know that the labor of baking is more complex and sophisticated than the labor of cooking, and consequently it expresses more the unique ability of man to prepare his food (see below 6:10, regarding the law of a cooked dish whose level is lower than that of a baked good).</p><p>As with bread, so with man: all the animals in the world learn in a short time and in a natural way how to walk, how to obtain their food and how to be fruitful and multiply, whereas man needs many years to learn to obtain food for himself, clothing, shelter from cold and heat, and to establish a family. He must learn everything, but after he has learned and contemplated, he is able to harness the enormous forces of nature to his service: to build houses from stones and earth, to heat them with wood and oil; to sow fields and plant trees, to produce food in abundance and manufacture various garments; to pave roads, to manufacture cars, ships and airplanes, in order to transport people and goods in them.</p><p>Animals hardly need to learn how to communicate with members of their species, whereas man needs many years in order to learn to speak, to understand facial expressions and the various emotions, but after he has learned this, his relationships with his family members and friends will be full of endless meaning. Animals have no moral questions about their lives and about the nature around them; they fit in. Whereas man hesitates, asks and struggles, years upon years, generations upon generations, and through this he is able to grasp abstract concepts of good and evil, truth and falsehood, faith and destiny.</p><p>One can say that all the animals in the world, together with the fact that they have no free choice and have no ability to create things in the world, also eat simple foods as they are in nature. But man, who has understanding and choice, and on whom rests the responsibility to repair the world, his food too is created through a long process that requires understanding. Therefore it is specifically bread that sustains the heart of man, as it is stated (Tehillim 104:15): \"and <b>bread</b> that sustains the heart of man.\"</p><p>Seemingly, after man has toiled so much in preparing the bread, he could become proud and think as if he alone brought forth the bread from the earth. In order that we not err in this, the Sages instituted the blessing: \"Blessed are You, Lord our God, King of the universe, who brings forth bread from the earth.\" For in truth, all the special qualities in the five species of grain and in the earth, as well as our understanding and our ability to make bread, all come from Him, may He be blessed, and therefore in truth it is He who brought forth the bread from the earth. And when a person forgets the One who spoke and the world came into being, he is gradually drawn after his lowly sides, after his evil desires. Then all his great talents become a stumbling block for him, and he uses all his great enterprises to do evil, to destroy and to devastate. Instead of adding life and blessing, he causes death and decay. Only if he remembers the Lord his God, and the wondrous destiny placed upon him, will he be able to direct all his talents for the good, to add life to the world, and to be a partner with God in its repair and its building.</p><p>Therefore the blessing over bread is so important, for it connects the food, in which human creative power is expressed, to its Divine source. And by virtue of the blessing, eating bread gives us strength to understand and to comprehend, to keep and to do, all the words of the Torah.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"10-03-02","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-03-02/","title_he":"ב – הידורים בברכה על הלחם","title_en":"Enhancements in the Blessing over Bread","body_en":"<p>When a person comes to recite a blessing over a food, it is proper that he hold it in his right hand, for thereby the blessing will be said with concentration and respect (as explained below 9:4). And when he comes to recite the blessing over bread, because of its special importance, it is proper that he hold it during the blessing with both hands and with all ten fingers, to allude to the ten mitzvot involved in making bread (SA 167:4; MB 24).</p><p>As is known, the more important and vital a matter we are engaged in, the more one can rise through it or, Heaven forbid, fall. And so that we will rise and not fall, the Torah gave us mitzvot to guide us. And the more important the matter, the more mitzvot there are concerning it. Therefore many mitzvot accompany married life and the service of the Holy Temple. And so too regarding the food that sustains a person, and especially bread, for many mitzvot accompany the process of its preparation: 1) When plowing, we were commanded not to plow with an ox and a donkey together. 2) When sowing, we were commanded not to sow kil'ayim (forbidden mixtures). 3) When reaping, we were commanded to leave leket, shikh'ḥa and pe'a (gleanings, forgotten sheaves and the corner of the field) for the poor. 4) When threshing with the help of an animal, we were commanded not to cause it suffering by muzzling its mouth so that it cannot eat. 5) The first fruit of the new grain we were commanded to bring as bikkurim (first fruits) to the kohanim in the Temple. 6) After reaping the wheat, we were commanded to separate teruma and terumat ma'aser for the kohanim; 7) ma'aser rishon (first tithe) for the Levites; 8) ma'aser sheni (second tithe), which must be eaten in sanctity in Jerusalem (in years 1, 2, 4 and 5 of the shemita cycle); 9) and ma'aser ani (the tithe for the poor) for the poor (in years 3 and 6).<br />10) When kneading the dough, we were commanded to separate ḥalla for the kohen.</p><p>One who intends to eat from two kinds of bread should recite the blessing over the more important bread. This is the law for all blessings over enjoyment: if he had, for example, two apples, it is preferable that he say the blessing over the better of them, for in this way the blessing will express the praise in the best way. All the more so is it proper to act this way with bread, and therefore many detailed laws were stated regarding the order of importance and precedence among the various kinds of bread, and the laws of precedence for other foods were learned from it (below 9:9).</p><p>This is the order of importance: wheat bread precedes barley bread, because it comes first in the order of the seven species, and also its taste is finer.</p><p>One who wishes to eat from a small whole roll and from a large loaf of bread that has already begun to be sliced should preferably recite the blessing over the roll, because it is whole whereas the loaf has already been cut, and the advantage of wholeness takes precedence over the advantage of size.</p><p>One who wishes to eat whole-wheat bread and also bread from sifted (white) flour: in the past the ruling was to recite the blessing over the bread from sifted flour, which was considered better. However, today many consider whole-wheat bread to be better. Therefore, one who prefers whole-wheat bread should recite the blessing over it. And one who does not prefer either of them should recite the blessing over the bread from sifted flour.</p><p>When there are two breads of equal value, and one is larger than the other, it is preferable to recite the blessing over the larger one, because it is more important (SA 168:1-4; MB 15; SA 211:1-2; MB 33).<sup>1</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":1,"en":"The MB 168:15 explained that this is the rule: <b>1)</b> One should give precedence to one of the seven species over other species, and also among the seven species one should give precedence to the one mentioned earlier in the verse; therefore wheat precedes barley, and barley precedes the other species of grain. <b>2)</b> The whole precedes the cut. <b>3)</b> Clean and sifted bread precedes bread that is not sifted. <b>4)</b> The large precedes the small. When there is a conflict between the rules, the earlier rule takes precedence over the later one. There is an additional rule: to give precedence to the one that is more beloved (preferred). When there is a conflict between the preferred and the important according to the rules, then when the importance stems from the first two rules, one of the seven species or whole, according to the majority of poskim one of the seven species or a whole item precedes the preferred, as explained in SA 211:1-2 and MB 211:4. According to the Rambam, the preferred takes precedence. And when there is a conflict between the preferred and the third or fourth rule, it seems that the preferred takes precedence. See below 9:8-10 regarding the order of precedence among other foods."}]},{"slug":"10-03-03","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-03-03/","title_he":"ג – בציעת הלחם ושלא להפסיק בין הברכה לאכילה","title_en":"Breaking the Bread, and Not Interrupting Between the Blessing and Eating","body_en":"<p>Since a whole bread is more honored, it is proper to recite the blessing over it while it is still whole. But on the other hand, it is right to attach the Ha-motzi blessing to the eating, and if we recite the blessing over a whole bread we will be forced to delay after the blessing until it is cut. Therefore the Sages said that before the blessing one should begin to cut the bread, in such a way that it still remains attached, so that if he holds it by the small part he can lift all of it. In this state the bread is still considered whole, and at the same time, immediately after the blessing he will be able to finish breaking it and taste from it.</p><p>All this refers to bread whose cutting takes some time, but one who comes to eat a roll or a pita, which is easily and quickly broken, should not begin to break it before the blessing, but only after it (SA 167:1, and BHL s.v. \"ve-tzarikh\").</p><p>On Shabbat one should not begin to cut the ḥalla before the blessing, because on Shabbat there is a mitzva to recite the blessing specifically over two whole ḥallot, and if he begins to cut, the ḥalla will no longer be whole (see Rema 167:1; many wrote that one should mark the place of breaking: Baḥ, Maharshal, MB 274:5).</p><p>One should break the bread at a place where it is well baked, so that the praise of the blessing will apply to the choicest place in the bread. Today breads are baked uniformly, and one may break at any place in the bread (SA 167:1).<sup>2</sup></p><p>Immediately after the blessing one should eat from the bread. And if he transgressed and spoke, even one word, before he began to eat, since he interrupted with speech, he has lost the blessing and must recite the blessing again in order to eat. But if he transgressed and spoke after he had put the food into his mouth, he should not recite Ha-motzi again (see below 9:3).</p><p>One who speaks between the blessing and eating about a subject related to the food, for example, he recited the blessing over the bread and asked that salt or a knife be brought to him, or asked that bread be brought for a guest, although he acted improperly, since his speech was about a subject related to the eating, this speech does not interpose between the blessing and the eating, and he does not need to recite the blessing again (SA OḤ 167:6).</p><p>It is good to eat a kezayit immediately after the blessing without interrupting, in order to give honor to the Ha-motzi blessing. However, when there is a need, one may speak immediately after swallowing a little of the bread.<sup>3</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":2,"en":"Some take into account the various opinions regarding the definition of the choicest place, and are scrupulous to break off a little from the upper part of the bread and a little from the lower part (see Rema and MB 167:3). It should be added that although in industrial baking all places in the bread are equal, it is good to taste first also from the hard crust that envelops the bread, for the hard crust (for one who is able to eat it) is considered choicer than the soft part."},{"n":3,"en":"It is good to eat a kezayit without interrupting, as cited in MB 167:15 and 35. This is a special law for Ha-motzi, both because of the honor of the blessing, and because of the Al Netilat Yadayim blessing according to some of the poskim (above 2:6), and also in order to establish a meal on the bread and exempt the other foods (Dagul Me-revava; below, halakha 6)."}]},{"slug":"10-03-04","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-03-04/","title_he":"ד – אימתי טוב שאחד יוציא את חבריו בברכת 'המוציא'?","title_en":"When Is It Good for One Person to Fulfill the Obligation of His Companions with the Ha-motzi Blessing?","body_en":"<p>Two people who came to eat bread together, since they have set themselves to eat together, it is good that one recite the blessing aloud and exempt his companion, because a blessing said on behalf of two people is more honorable. And since the listener fulfills his obligation with the blessing he hears from his companion, he may not interrupt with speech until he himself eats.<sup>4</sup> But if each one is eating for himself, and they are not waiting for each other, each one must recite the blessing for himself (SA 167:11; see below 5:2).</p><p>Accordingly, it is understood why on Shabbatot, when the whole family eats together, they wait until everyone has finished washing their hands, and then the head of the household recites Ha-motzi aloud, and those seated answer \"Amen\" after him and fulfill their obligation through his blessing. For since they have set themselves to eat together, the enhanced practice is that one person recite Ha-motzi for all. And each of those seated must be careful not to speak until he himself eats.</p><p>At a meal with many participants, even though everyone gathered to eat together, it is preferable that at each table one person recite the blessing for his companions who washed their hands with him, because if they wait for all the participants, too great an interruption will be created between the washing and the blessing.<sup>5</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":4,"en":"If he spoke before he himself ate and after the one reciting the blessing had eaten, he must recite the blessing himself. And although some hold that he should not recite the blessing (Rema 167:6; Ben Ish Ḥai, Emor 16), the opinion of most poskim is that he should recite the blessing (among them: Tosafot, the Rosh, Beit Yosef, Magen Avraham, Birkei Yosef, MB 167:43 and Kaf Ha-ḥayim 58), and this is the practice of many."},{"n":5,"en":"It is difficult to set definitions for what is a meal with many participants, and everything depends on the circumstances, for there is an advantage in one person reciting the blessing for all, and there is an advantage in not waiting a long time between the washing and the blessing and the eating (see MB 165:5). When people eat fruit and the like together, according to SA it is good that one recite the blessing for all, and according to the Rema it is preferable that each one recite the blessing for himself, as explained below 12:7. And see MB 213:11-12."}]},{"slug":"10-03-05","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-03-05/","title_he":"ה – אם צריך לטבול את הפת במלח","title_en":"Whether One Must Dip the Bread in Salt","body_en":"<p>In the past people were accustomed to bake bread without salt, and during the meal they would season the bread with salt, salad or another food. Therefore the Sages said that after the Ha-motzi blessing it is proper to eat the bread in the choicest manner, and thereby emphasize the praise of the blessing; therefore people were accustomed to flavor the bread eaten after the blessing with salt or another food. And one who wished to eat the bread as it is, dry, without salt and other foods, did not need to dip the bread in salt.</p><p>Today our breads are baked with salt and spices, so it turns out that our bread is considered choice, and according to the law one may recite the blessing over it as it is, without flavoring it with a little salt or salad (SA 167:5). Nevertheless, many are accustomed to the enhancement of dipping the bread in salt or salad, so that the eating after the blessing will be in the choicest manner.</p><p>There is a further enhancement: to place salt on the table during the meal, like the mitzva in the Temple to add salt to the sacrifices, as it is stated (Vayikra 2:13): \"With all your offerings you shall offer salt.\" And we thereby allude that the table is like the altar, and the food like a sacrifice, for it gives a person strength for the service of God (Rema, ibid.).</p><p>Some are accustomed, according to Kabbala, to dip the bread in salt (Magen Avraham 167:15; MB 33). The reason for this is that bread alludes to the attribute of ḥesed (kindness), through which a person is nourished and lives, whereas salty salt alludes to the harsh attribute of din (judgment), which, when dominion is given to it, the world is destroyed. And when we dip the bread in salt, we sweeten the salt by making it secondary to the bread, for then it joins the nourishing kindness in the bread, and instead of harming, it benefits. People are accustomed to be scrupulous about this mainly on Shabbatot and Yamim Tovim.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"10-03-06","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-03-06/","title_he":"ו – על איזה מאכלים ומשקים מברכים בסעודה","title_en":"Which Foods and Drinks Require a Blessing During the Meal","body_en":"<p>Bread is the main food of a person, and so we find with Ya'akov our forefather, when fleeing from Esav, that he prayed for bread, as it is stated (Bereishit 28:20): \"And Ya'akov made a vow, saying: If God will be with me and will guard me on this way that I am going, and will give me <b>bread</b> to eat and clothing to wear.\" And so we find at the time of the famine in Egypt, when the Egyptians turned to Yosef, they said (Bereishit 47:15): \"Give us bread; why should we die before you?\" and they were prepared to sell all that they had for bread. And likewise when Yosef provided for his father's household, everything he gave them was called bread, as it is stated (ibid. 12): \"And Yosef sustained his father and his brothers and all his father's household with bread, according to the little ones.\" And likewise it is stated about King Belshatzar, who made a great feast: \"made a great feast [lit., bread]\" (Daniel 5:1).</p><p>Thus all of a person's food is called bread; therefore the Ha-motzi blessing exempts all the foods eaten during the meal, such as meat, fish, potatoes, rice, lentils, cheeses, vegetable salad, cooked salads and the like. And even if he eats them by themselves, without bread, the Ha-motzi blessing exempts them. For the Ha-motzi blessing is not directed only toward the bread and what is actually eaten with it, but toward all the foods meant to satisfy. For the main aim of bread is to satisfy, and therefore everything eaten for the sake of satiation is considered secondary to the bread and is exempted by its blessing. It must be emphasized that in order for the bread to exempt the other foods even when they are eaten by themselves, one must eat a kezayit of bread before eating the other foods by themselves.</p><p>However, foods meant for dessert, that is, those foods that a person usually eats because of their good taste and not in order to be satisfied, such as dates, grapes, watermelon and the like, are not exempted by the Ha-motzi blessing. For the Ha-motzi blessing applies only to foods meant for satiation, which are the essence of the meal, but desserts, which are eaten to add good taste, and which people usually eat at the end of meals or between them, are an addition to the meal, and one must recite a separate blessing over them.</p><p>Therefore, one who eats tree fruits during his meal recites Ha-etz, and one who eats watermelon recites Ha-adama, and one who eats ice cream or pudding recites Shehakol. All this concerns the first blessing, but one does not need to recite a final blessing over them, because Birkat Ha-mazon exempts all the foods eaten during the meal, whether for satiation or for dessert (SA 177:1).<sup>6</sup></p><p>Drinks that a person usually drinks during his meal, such as water, juice and alcoholic drinks, are exempted by the Ha-motzi blessing. But wine, because of its importance, is not exempted by the Ha-motzi blessing (below 7:3).</p><p>Coffee and tea that people usually drink after the food are considered part of the meal and are exempted by the Ha-motzi blessing. But alcoholic drinks that are drunk after the food are not part of the meal, and one recites a blessing over them.<sup>7</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":6,"en":"Berakhot 41b: \"Rav Pappa said: The halakha is that things that come because of the meal, during the meal, require a blessing neither before them nor after them. And things that do not come because of the meal, during the meal, require a blessing before them but do not require a blessing after them.\" According to Rashi, things that come because of the meal means that they are eaten with the bread, and therefore they are exempted by its blessing. According to the other Rishonim, all foods meant for satiation are included in the Ha-motzi blessing (Meiri). The Ritva explained this on the basis of an expansion of the principle of ikar and tafel (primary and secondary): all satiating foods become secondary and are drawn after the bread. SA 177:1 ruled according to their words, and added in se'if 4 that even if one did not have them in mind when reciting Ha-motzi, the Ha-motzi blessing exempts them. However, the Aḥaronim were in doubt as to how much bread one must eat for it to be considered primary; see MB 177:3. Most Aḥaronim agreed that eating a kezayit is sufficient (Dagul Me-revava; Eshel Avraham [Buczacz] 167:1; Igrot Moshe OḤ 4:41)."},{"n":7,"en":"Wine is not exempted by Ha-motzi, as explained in Berakhot 41b and below 7:3. Regarding other drinks, according to the Behag, the Rid and others, the Ha-motzi blessing does not exempt drinking, and even over water one must recite Shehakol. Only the blessing over wine exempts other drinks, as on Shabbat, when the blessing over the wine of Kiddush exempts the other drinks that will be brought later in the meal. In contrast, according to Rabbeinu Tam, the Ri, the Rashba, Talmidei Rabbeinu Yona and many others, drinking is drawn after eating, and therefore the Ha-motzi blessing exempts drinks. This is the opinion of the majority of poskim, and this is the custom (there were scrupulous people who were accustomed, as cited in SA 174:7, to drink less than the minimum amount before the meal; and see Ben Ish Ḥai, Naso 5). Regarding coffee after the food, according to the Ḥayei Adam 43:11, one must recite Shehakol, because it is not part of the meal. And some say that one should not recite a blessing over it because it is secondary to the meal, and this is the custom (see MB 174:39; Birkat Hashem vol. 3, 10, note 261). Regarding alcoholic drinks during the meal, according to the Maharshal and the Levush, they require a blessing, and according to the Magen Avraham and the Shulḥan Arukh Ha-Rav, they do not require a blessing, because they are meant to arouse the appetite and consequently they are part of the meal. But after the meal one must recite a blessing over them (in a situation of doubt one should not recite the blessing, and one can resolve the doubt with a blessing over wine, which exempts all drinks. And when one makes Kiddush, the blessing over the wine exempts all the drinks one drinks until Birkat Ha-mazon. See MB 174:39, and BHL s.v. \"ve-ha-minhag\")."}]},{"slug":"10-03-07","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-03-07/","title_he":"ז – פרטי דיני פירות בסעודה","title_en":"Details of the Laws of Fruit During the Meal","body_en":"<p>All fruits and vegetables eaten together with foods meant for satiation, whether raw or cooked, are exempted by the Ha-motzi blessing. This includes prunes cooked with meat, raisins in rice, a sweet stew of prunes and apricots (tzimmes), and carrot salad and Waldorf salad served with the courses meant for satiation. But if a sweet carrot salad or Waldorf salad was served after the meal for dessert, one must recite a first blessing over them. Likewise over compote, that is, fruit cooked with sugar, which is served at the end of the meal, one recites a first blessing.</p><p>When grapefruit or melon is served at the beginning of the meal, one must recite a first blessing over them, because they are a kind of dessert served at the beginning of the meal in order to open the meal with a good taste, and they are not an actual part of the meal. But foods served at the beginning of the meal in order to arouse the appetite, such as various pickles, are part of the meal, and the Ha-motzi blessing exempts them.</p><p>One who intends that one of the courses meant for satiation will consist of fruit, for example grapes: as long as he eats them together with the bread, the grapes are secondary to the bread, and the blessing over the bread exempts them. But if he intends to eat the grapes also by themselves, without bread, there is a doubt whether he will need to recite a blessing over them. In order to avoid the doubt, it is preferable that right at the outset he eat grapes by themselves, and then according to all opinions he recites Ha-etz over them (MB 177:10).<sup>8</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":8,"en":"One who intends that fruits (or other foods) that are generally eaten for dessert will be the only food that joins the bread in order to satisfy him should eat them first with bread and not recite a blessing. Indeed, according to the Rosh, even if he eats them without bread he does not recite a blessing, since the aim of eating them is satiation. But according to Talmidei Rabbeinu Yona, since people are not accustomed to eat them for satiation, only if he begins to eat them with bread will he show thereby that he intends to eat them for satiation, and afterward he may continue to eat them by themselves without a blessing. In order to satisfy all the poskim, this is how it is proper to act (SA 177:3)."}]},{"slug":"10-03-08","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-03-08/","title_he":"ח – דין עוגות ומזונות בסעודה","title_en":"The Law of Cakes and Mezonot During the Meal","body_en":"<p>Three opinions were stated regarding eating Mezonot baked goods that are meant for dessert during the meal. Some say that one does not recite Mezonot over them, since they resemble bread and they too are satiating, and consequently they are part of the meal and are exempted by the Ha-motzi blessing (Rashba). On the other hand, some say that since they are eaten for dessert and not for satiation, they are not part of the meal, and therefore the Ha-motzi blessing does not exempt them and one must recite Mezonot over them (Tosafot). And some say that the matter is in doubt, and since in cases of doubt regarding blessings we are lenient, one who eats them during the meal should not recite a blessing (see SA 168:7). Those who wish to be scrupulous and avoid the doubt should preferably refrain from eating Mezonot during the meal (Ḥida).</p><p>In practice, those who wish to eat Mezonot during the meal must decide for themselves how they relate to eating them. If they decide that it is part of the meal, they should resolve in their minds that the Ha-motzi blessing will always exempt all the Mezonot they eat during the meal, and consequently they will not recite Mezonot over them. And if they decide that eating Mezonot is meant for dessert, they should resolve in their minds that the Ha-motzi blessing will never exempt Mezonot, and they will recite Mezonot over them. And as long as they have not decided, because of the doubt they should not recite Mezonot before them, and if they wish to be scrupulous, they should refrain from eating them during the meal.<sup>9</sup></p><p>Over Mezonot items that are cooked or deep-fried in oil and served for dessert, such as Bissli, since they do not resemble bread, for bread is baked whereas they are cooked, if they are eaten for dessert one recites Mezonot over them. But over sufganiyot (doughnuts) served at the end of the meal for dessert, although they are cooked in oil, one should not recite Mezonot. For some hold that they are exempted by the Ha-motzi blessing, because their dough resembles bread (below 6, note 9). In addition, they are very satiating, and consequently eating them joins the aim of the meal, and all the foods of the meal are exempted by the Ha-motzi blessing. But since there is doubt about this, le-khatḥila it is preferable to eat them outside the meal, and to recite Mezonot and Al Ha-miḥya over them.</p>","notes_en":[{"n":9,"en":"See below 6:2, that there are three explanations of what pat ha-ba'a be-kisnin is (filled, kneaded [with other liquids], or crunchy), and according to many (Tosafot, the Mordekhai, the Shibbolei Ha-leket, Magen Avraham 168:22, Taz 10, Ma'amar Mordekhai), in all three types, if one eats them during the meal for dessert, one recites Mezonot. And some poskim instruct this way in practice (Rav Eliyahu). In contrast, according to the Rashba (Berakhot 41b) and several other Rishonim, in any case one should not recite a blessing during the meal over any type of pat ha-ba'a be-kisnin, that is, a Mezonot baked good, for since it is satiating, it joins the aim of the meal and is exempted by the Ha-motzi blessing. (And likewise we find that when one eats pat ha-ba'a be-kisnin in the amount of a fixed meal, its blessing is Ha-motzi; if so, there is room to say that also when one eats it during the meal it joins the fixed meal and its blessing is Ha-motzi.) There is another central opinion: that the three interpretations of pat ha-ba'a be-kisnin are in dispute, and if so, over every type of Mezonot baked good that one eats for dessert, some say one should recite Mezonot and some say Ha-motzi. Therefore, when one eats them by themselves, because of the doubt one recites Mezonot, and when one eats them during the meal, they are exempted, because of the doubt, by the Ha-motzi blessing. And over a baked good in which all three interpretations are fulfilled together (for example, baklava), one must recite Mezonot during the meal (SA 168:7, as explained by the Dagul Me-revava, the Ḥayei Adam, Rabbi Akiva Eger, and BHL 168:8, s.v. \"te'unim\"). In practice, many wrote that because of the doubt, one should not recite Mezonot during the meal over a baked good that does not include all three interpretations together (and so ruled the Shevet Ha-Levi and Rav Ovadia Yosef). In order to avoid the doubt, it is right not to eat Mezonot during the meal (Ḥida). And if one eats them, it is good that he explicitly intend to exempt them with Ha-motzi (Ḥayei Adam 43:9). He may likewise exempt himself from the doubt with cooked Mezonot that are eaten for dessert (for example, Bissli). The Or Le-Tziyon (vol. 2, 12:10, in the note) wrote another good piece of advice: that when he recites Ha-motzi he should explicitly intend not to exempt the Mezonot that will be served for dessert during the meal, and when cakes are served to him, he should recite Mezonot over them. And see the Harḥavot. In what I wrote above as halakha, I included the three opinions: that the most enhanced practice is to avoid every doubt and not eat Mezonot baked goods during the meal. And if he wishes to eat them and has not decided what they are for him, he should not recite a blessing. But the right thing is that he decide on a fixed basis whether they are dessert, and recite Mezonot, or part of the satiation of the meal, and not recite a blessing."}]},{"slug":"10-03-09","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-03-09/","title_he":"ט – לאחר הסעודה","title_en":"After the Meal","body_en":"<p>In the days of the Sages there was a custom to end the meal completely, and since they intended to continue sitting together, they would delay reciting Birkat Ha-mazon, and if more foods were served to them, eating them was considered an eating completely separate from the meal, and therefore they would recite a blessing over it before and after. And afterward they would still complete their obligation with Birkat Ha-mazon.</p><p>However, today we are not accustomed to end the meal completely before Birkat Ha-mazon. For as long as we have not yet recited Birkat Ha-mazon, there is still a possibility that we will eat from the bread (SA 177:2). Even if they changed the tablecloth, since it is still possible that they will want to eat more of the bread, we are still considered within the meal. Likewise at weddings, when the waiters clear the tables in order to finish their work, the guests do not wish to end the meal until Birkat Ha-mazon, and it is even possible that they may want to eat more of the bread or other foods if they are brought before them. Therefore, in our days there is no law of 'after the meal'. And if foods are served for dessert, one recites a blessing before them and not after them (as explained above, halakhot 6-7).<sup>10</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":10,"en":"According to Tosafot, the Rosh, the Mordekhai and others (on Berakhot 41b), 'after the meal' is when they decided to withdraw their hands from the bread. According to Talmidei Rabbeinu Yona, the Rashba and the Orḥot Ḥayim, it is when they removed the table from before them (one can say that they do not disagree, but rather removing the table is the proof that they do not intend to eat any more of the bread; and see BHL 177:2, s.v. \"she-ein,\" that the Rishonim disagreed whether over all foods after the meal one recites a blessing before and after). However, in practice many Rishonim wrote that the law of 'after the meal' is not common among us (Tosafot, the Rashba, Talmidei Rabbeinu Yona, the Rosh and many others). Nevertheless, the Beit Yosef wrote in explaining the opinion of the Tur, and this is also the opinion of the Levush and those who agree with him, that at large meals the law of 'after the meal' is common. But the Baḥ wrote that even at large meals there is no law of 'after the meal', and so he learned from SA 177:2, which did not mention a distinction between small and large meals. And the Magen Avraham 7 wrote, based on Talmidei Rabbeinu Yona, that since today there is no removal of the table, even though they have finished eating there is no law of 'after the meal'. Some say that removing the tablecloth today is considered like removing the table in the past (Birkat Hashem vol. 3, 10, note 264), and some say that it is not like removing the table (Or Le-Tziyon vol. 2, 12:13). And according to the Kaf Ha-ḥayim 14, even if they remove the table there is no law of 'after the meal' today. And it seems to me, in my humble opinion, that after it was ruled, because of the doubt, that there is no law of 'after the meal', consequently it has become settled in our minds that at our meals, even if they are very prolonged, we do not end the meal completely until Birkat Ha-mazon, and consequently one can say with certainty that the law of 'after the meal' does not apply to them."}]},{"slug":"10-03-10","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-03-10/","title_he":"י – עד היכן מועילה ברכת 'המוציא'","title_en":"How Far the Ha-motzi Blessing Is Effective","body_en":"<p>The Ha-motzi blessing over bread exempts all the foods and drinks that people usually eat during a meal. And even if the meal continues for many hours, the Ha-motzi blessing recited at the outset exempts all the foods eaten during the meal.</p><p>One who decided definitively to end his meal, and then changed his mind and wanted to eat more, the poskim disagreed whether he must recite a blessing before he continues eating. In order to avoid the doubt, it is right that he refrain from continuing to eat. And if after Birkat Ha-mazon he wishes to eat and drink, he should recite blessings before and after them.<sup>11</sup></p><p>If he has already washed his hands for mayim aḥaronim (the final waters), he is considered as one who has begun Birkat Ha-mazon, and even if he changes his mind and wishes to eat more, he should first recite Birkat Ha-mazon, and if he afterward wishes to eat and drink, he should recite blessings before and after them (SA 179:1).<sup>12</sup></p><p>A guest who thought that the meal had ended and had already made up his mind not to eat any more, and afterward more foods were brought to him, since he relies on the decision of the host, according to all opinions he may continue eating without reciting a blessing. And the same applies to a man who relies on his wife in matters of food, and likewise those who eat in a dining hall, who rely on the kitchen managers. But if the guest made up his mind that even if more foods were brought to him he would not eat any more of them, and afterward he changed his mind and wanted to eat more, it is right that he refrain from eating and drinking, and rather recite Birkat Ha-mazon (SA 179:2).</p><p>One who interrupted in the middle of his meal to pray does not need to recite a blessing again when he returns to eat, because he did not divert his attention from continuing to eat (SA 178:6). One who dozed during his meal, even for an hour, this is not considered an interruption, and when he continues eating he should not recite a blessing again. But if he went to sleep a regular sleep on his bed for more than half an hour, this is like one who diverted his attention from eating, and when he returns to eat he will need to wash his hands with a blessing and recite a blessing before eating (SA 178:7; MB 48; and see above 2:16, note 11).</p><p>One who intended to eat from the foods he had at home, and friends came in the middle of the meal and brought him additional foods, or after he finished what he had at home he changed his mind and asked that additional foods be brought to him from the store or from the neighbors, must recite a blessing over those foods, because he certainly did not have them in mind when he recited Ha-motzi (MB 174:18).</p>","notes_en":[{"n":11,"en":"According to the Rambam, and so it is implied by the Rosh (see BHL 179:1, s.v. \"ve-afilu\"), since he decided to end his eating, the blessing he recited at the outset no longer helps him, and if he wishes to continue eating, he must recite a blessing again before he eats. According to Talmidei Rabbeinu Yona and the Ran, since it is common for a person to decide to stop eating and then change his mind, one who wishes to continue eating does not need to recite a blessing before eating. And although most poskim hold like the Rambam, there is doubt about this, and therefore in practice one who continues to eat should not recite a blessing. But le-khatḥila it is preferable not to continue eating, so as not to enter into doubt (see BHL 179:1, s.v. \"ein\"). Today there is no difference between drinking and eating, and even one who decides not to drink any more may still change his mind, and because of the doubt he cannot recite a blessing (see Harḥavot). Only if he was compelled to stop his meal, for example a taxi is waiting for him outside, is he considered as one who has firmly decided not to eat or drink any more."},{"n":12,"en":"If he washed his hands for mayim aḥaronim, according to several poskim (Tosafot, the Rosh, the Tur, Rabbeinu Yeruḥam and others, as cited in Sha'ar Ha-tziyun 179:5-6), if he changed his mind to continue eating, he should recite a first blessing and eat. And in this there would seemingly be no doubt of a blessing in vain, since even Talmidei Rabbeinu Yona and the Ran would agree that after washing the hands he has firmly decided to stop his meal. However, the Baḥ and the Magen Avraham wrote that one should not act this way, and so wrote the BHL (179:1, s.v. \"ad\"), because according to several of the great Rishonim (Rashi, the Rashbam, the Or Zaru'a, the Rambam, the Ra'avad, the Rashba, the Ra'ah), after he has washed his hands he is considered as one who has accepted upon himself to begin Birkat Ha-mazon, and therefore he is forbidden to continue eating even if he recites a first blessing before eating."}]},{"slug":"10-03-11","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-03-11/","title_he":"יא – אם מותר לצאת מהבית באמצע הסעודה","title_en":"Whether One May Leave the House in the Middle of the Meal","body_en":"<p>Le-khatḥila one should not leave the house in the middle of the meal, because some hold that whoever leaves his house has interrupted his meal, and if he wishes to continue eating, he must first recite Birkat Ha-mazon over what he ate, and afterward Ha-motzi over what he intends to eat (Rambam; SA 178:2). However, be-di'avad, if he left his house in the middle of his meal after already having eaten a kezayit of bread, he may continue his meal without reciting Ha-motzi, for be-di'avad we rely on the opinion of those who hold that leaving the house is not considered an interruption for a bread meal. The same applies to one who needed to go out in the middle of his meal for the sake of a mitzva or for another urgent need: if it is clear to him that afterward he will return to continue his meal, he may go out without reciting Birkat Ha-mazon, and when he returns he should continue his meal without reciting Ha-motzi, and when he finishes he should recite Birkat Ha-mazon (Rosh; Rema 178:2). But if when he left the house he completely diverted his attention from the meal, for example he did not intend to continue the meal and also remained outside the place of the meal for more than an hour, if he wishes to continue eating, he will need to wash his hands and recite Ha-motzi (SA 170:1; MB 9).<sup>13</sup></p><p>If one of the diners remains in the house until the end of the meal, according to all opinions the other participants may leave the house in the middle of the meal when there is a need. And when they return to the meal, according to all opinions they will not need to recite Ha-motzi again, because the one who remained in the house at the place of the meal continues the continuity of the meal on behalf of those who went out (SA 178:2).</p><p>Travelers: the entire road is their place, and therefore they may begin eating in one place and continue eating on their way, and the blessing they recited at the outset is effective for them for the continuation of the journey (SA 178:4).</p><p>When there is a need, a person may intend, with the Ha-motzi blessing, to begin his meal in one place and continue it afterward in another place. This is what people do when they wish to join a Sheva Berakhot meal after the meal, or when they wish to end the meal at a gathering of friends.<sup>14</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":13,"en":"Pesaḥim 101b: according to the opinion of Rav Sheshet as understood by the Rambam, one who left his place in the middle of his meal and afterward wanted to continue eating must first recite Birkat Ha-mazon over what he ate and afterward Ha-motzi over what he intends to eat, for by leaving his place he ended his meal and became obligated in Birkat Ha-mazon. And so it was ruled in SA 178:1-2. In contrast, the opinion of Rav Ḥisda is that since over a bread meal one must recite Birkat Ha-mazon in the place where he ate, one who uproots himself from his place after eating a kezayit of bread has not cancelled his original fixed establishment, and therefore as long as he has not completely diverted his attention from the meal, he may continue eating without a blessing, whether in the previous place or in the new place. And so ruled the Rashbam, Tosafot, the Rosh and the Rema. And since this dispute touches on a doubt regarding blessings, many of the later Sephardic poskim ruled in practice like the Rema, and so wrote the Ben Ish Ḥai, Beha'alotkha 2; Kaf Ha-ḥayim 178:14. Le-khatḥila one should be careful not to leave the house in the middle of the meal, first in order to take into account the opinion of the SA, and second, even according to the Rema one should not leave le-khatḥila, lest he forget to recite the blessing. Regarding going out to other rooms in the same house, see below 9:7. The rule is that to rooms to which people are accustomed to go in the middle of the meal one may go le-khatḥila, and to rooms to which people are not accustomed to go, le-khatḥila one should not go, but when there is a need one may go. Le-khatḥila, one who intends to go out for half an hour or more, even though he intends to return and continue eating, if he is not in a hurry, it is right that he recite Birkat Ha-mazon before leaving, and after he returns he should wash his hands again and recite Ha-motzi. But if he goes out for a short time, he should not recite the blessing, for according to those who hold like Rav Ḥisda this would be an unnecessary blessing (MB 178:35; BHL s.v. \"be-lo\"). And if one of his companions remained there and is waiting for him, even if he goes out for more than half an hour he should not recite the blessing. And see below 10:13."},{"n":14,"en":"There is room to say that the Rif and the Rambam would also agree that intention during Ha-motzi is effective for another place, and certainly this is the law according to Tosafot and the Rosh. And so wrote the Ben Ish Ḥai, Beha'alotkha 2; Yalkut Shemesh 75; Kaf Ha-ḥayim 178:32. Some were concerned that even intention during the Ha-motzi blessing is not effective according to the Rif and the Rambam, and one who wishes to eat in two places, when he finishes eating in the first place, should recite Birkat Ha-mazon, and wash his hands again and recite Ha-motzi in the second place; and so wrote the Ma'amar Mordekhai 9, Yalkut Yosef 178:1, Birkat Hashem vol. 3, 11:13. And it seems that one who wishes to take their opinion into account as well should follow the advice cited in the Or Le-Tziyon vol. 2, 12:15: that he should recite Ha-motzi near the entrance of his house, and only afterward sit down to eat at his table. For by beginning his meal at the entrance of his house, he himself showed that he is like a traveler, and consequently he will be permitted to continue his meal in another place."}]},{"slug":"10-03-12","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-03-12/","title_he":"יב – מאכלים שנאכלים לפני הסעודה","title_en":"Foods Eaten Before the Meal","body_en":"<p>One who wishes to eat a meal with bread should not eat or drink beforehand; rather, he should wash his hands and recite Ha-motzi, and afterward eat and drink everything he wishes. For if he eats or drinks before the meal, many times he causes an unnecessary blessing: if he could exempt what he wishes to eat with the Ha-motzi blessing, then by beginning to eat beforehand he caused himself to recite an additional blessing needlessly. Furthermore, after he eats and drinks he will enter into a doubt regarding the final blessing. For it is possible that he must recite a final blessing over what he ate before the meal, and on the other hand, perhaps it is better that he not recite a final blessing, since the Birkat Ha-mazon after the meal will also exempt what he ate before the meal.</p><p>And if he is compelled to wait a little until the meal, and in the meantime he wishes to eat or drink something, in order to avoid the doubt he must make a break of half an hour, or at least about a quarter of an hour, between finishing his eating and the beginning of the meal, and in this way, according to all opinions, he should recite a final blessing over what he ate or drank before the meal. Another option: after he eats what he wanted before the meal, he should recite a final blessing, and go outside for a very short walk, in order to create an interruption between his eating before the meal and the meal. This is how one should act at weddings where refreshments are served before the ḥuppa: they should recite a first and final blessing over the refreshments, and afterward the ḥuppa will be an interruption between the refreshments and the meal. Likewise at a brit mila, sometimes people first eat Mezonot and drink soft drinks, and afterward they wish to begin the meal; the right thing is that they make an interruption between eating the refreshments and the meal, and if it is not possible to wait about a quarter of an hour between them, it is good that they go out for a very short walk.</p><p>One who began to eat and drink and needs to begin the meal immediately, and cannot go out for a very short walk or wait a quarter of an hour, must know all the details of the laws explained in the note.<sup>15</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":15,"en":"<b>1)</b> If before the meal he ate meat, potatoes and other foods that people usually eat during a meal in order to be satisfied, and which are exempted by the Ha-motzi blessing, since he decided to eat them before the meal, he has made eating them an eating in its own right, and he must recite a final blessing over them. And if he forgot to recite the blessing before he began the meal, he must still complete the blessing. And if he has already recited Birkat Ha-mazon, because of the doubt he has lost the blessing. <b>2)</b> If he ate fruit before the meal, if he intends to continue eating fruit during the meal, he should not recite a final blessing over them. And if he does not intend to eat fruit during the meal, their law is like case 1. <b>3)</b> Over foods meant to open the appetite (such as wine and an alcoholic drink), because of the doubt he should not recite a final blessing. <b>4)</b> Over water he should recite a final blessing, and if he forgot to recite the blessing before the meal, if he intends to drink water during the meal, he should not recite a final blessing over it, and if he does not intend to drink during the meal, even after Birkat Ha-mazon he must complete the final blessing over the water. <b>5)</b> Over Mezonot baked goods regarding which there is doubt whether they are pat ha-ba'a be-kisnin (see above, note 9), because of the doubt he should not recite a final blessing. <b>6)</b> Over cooked Mezonot dishes such as ptitim, and Mezonot baked goods that according to all opinions are pat ha-ba'a be-kisnin, he should recite a final blessing, and if he has already begun the meal, he should not recite it. I wrote above a piece of advice: that one who ate or drank before the meal and is compelled to begin the meal, and there is doubt whether he is obligated to recite a final blessing, should go out for a very short walk, and thereby he will be able to recite a final blessing beforehand. Regarding foods that do not require a blessing in their place, leaving the place constitutes an interruption; see below 4:13; 9:7. And regarding foods that require a blessing in their place, although it was ruled in practice that leaving does not constitute an interruption, according to the Rambam and those who agree with him, it is considered an interruption (above, note 13). In addition, the opinion of the Rashba and those who agree with him is that Mezonot too do not require a blessing in their place, and regarding fruits of the seven species, more Rishonim hold that they do not require a blessing in their place (below 4:13; SA 178:5). If so, one may join those who hold that a change of place constitutes an interruption (and so wrote the Keren Le-David OḤ 59). Furthermore, even according to those who hold that for Mezonot and the seven species leaving does not constitute an interruption, here, where we are dealing with two different eatings, a walk, which is an engagement in something else, has more power to constitute an interruption and to obligate a final blessing over what he ate before the walk."}]}]});
